d leverage patterns corresponding to risk levels.\n\nbins = [0, 10, 40, float('inf')]\nlabels = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\ndf['CreditScore_Bin'] = pd.cut(df['CreditScore'], bins=bins, labels=labels)\nend\n\n### Drop Consideration ###\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\n# ZipCode is dropped because it does not inherently provide useful information for determining credit card application success. It is unlikely to correlate strongly with success since regional information without explicit socioeconomic encoding (e.g., median income per zipcode) is irrelevant.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# DriversLicense is dropped as it represents possession of a license and does not have a clear relationship with financial health or creditworthiness. It is likely noise in the model unless additional contextual factors (road safety concerns, regional mobility access) are considered.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n1. Debt-to-Income Ratio\npython\n# debt_to_income_ratio\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the ratio of Debt to Income. It captures an individual's financial burden relative to their earnings, assisting in understanding creditworthiness.\n# Usefulness: Debt-to-income ratio is a widely-used metric for evaluating an individual’s ability to manage debt repayment. Higher values may indicate financial strain, which could correlate with credit card application success rates.\n\ndf['debt_to_income_ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small value to Income to prevent division by zero.\nend\n\n2. Years Employed Categorization\npython\n# years_employed_bin\n# Feature description: Categorizes the 'YearsEmployed' feature into bins, such as Entry-level (<=2 years), Mid-level (2-5 years), and Senior-level (>5 years).\n# Usefulness: Helps transform the continuous 'YearsEmployed' into a more interpretable categorical feature that could better capture employment patterns related to credit card application success.\n\ndf['years_employed_bin'] = pd.cut(df['YearsEmployed'], bins=[-1, 2, 5, float('inf')], labels=['Entry-Level', 'Mid-Level', 'Senior-Level'])\nend\n\n3. Industry Group Target Encoding\npython\n# industry_target_encoding\n# Feature description: Applies target encoding to the 'Industry' categorical feature, replacing each category with the mean credit card application success rate for that group.\n# Usefulness: Industries might vary in credit card application success rates due to income patterns, job stability, or other factors. Encoding this information could help the model make more informed predictions.\n\ngroup_mean = df.groupby('Industry')['Answer'].mean()\ndf['industry_target_encoding'] = df['Industry'].map(group_mean)\nend\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns\n\nBelow are examples of columns that may be dropped if deemed irrelevant or redundant after feature engineering analysis.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# Reason: Whether someone has a driver's license might not directly impact their ability to successfully apply for a credit card. It shows limited predictive power in many financial datasets.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\n# Reason: The ZipCode column provides sparse locational data that might not offer significant differentiation for predicting credit card application success. It might also introduce noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n1. Debt-to-Income Ratio\npython\n# debt_to_income_ratio\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the ratio of Debt to Income. It measures the proportion of a person's income being used to manage debt.\n# Usefulness: Debt-to-income ratio is a standard measure of financial health. Higher ratios indicate potential financial strain, which is a critical factor in determining creditworthiness.\ndf['debt_to_income_ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small constant to prevent division by zero.\nend\n\n2. Employment Stability\npython\n# employment_stability\n# Feature description: This feature is created by multiplying the binary variable 'Employed' with 'YearsEmployed', capturing both employment status and duration.\n# Usefulness: This captures long-term employment stability. Individuals who are employed and have longer employment durations may be perceived as more stable and creditworthy.\ndf['employment_stability'] = df['Employed'] * df['YearsEmployed']\nend\n\n3. Marital and Income Interaction\npython\n# married_income_interaction\n# Feature description: This feature captures the interaction between 'Married' and 'Income' by multiplying the two variables. It reflects how income varies with marital status.\n# Usefulness: Married individuals with higher income may have better financial health due to shared income or lower financial risk perception. Combining these factors provides additional insight on financial stability.\ndf['married_income_interaction'] = df['Married'] * df['Income']\nend\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns\n\n1. Dropping 'DriversLicense'\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# Reason: The possession of a driver's license does not have a clear or direct relationship with financial stability or creditworthiness. It is likely to introduce noise into the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n2. Dropping 'ZipCode'\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\n# Reason: ZipCode provides location information, which is unlikely to offer predictive insights into credit card application success without more granular socioeconomic details.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach of these engineered features and column drops was selected to add interpretable and relevant financial insights into the model while removing irrelevant features that could harm performance."]###,
