fessional consistency, beneficial for understanding financial stability and creditworthiness.\n\ndf['Employment_Streak'] = df['Employed'] * (df['employment_stability_score']  1.1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns\n\n### 1. DriversLicense\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# The presence of a driver's license does not relate directly to financial stability or credit card application success. It is unlikely to add predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### 2. ZipCode\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\n# ZipCode is dropped because it provides locational data which is irrelevant for predicting credit card application success. Without socioeconomic enrichment, it serves as noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Feature Engineering - Next Three Steps\n\n#### 1. Feature: Debt-to-Income Ratio\npython\n# debt_to_income_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of Debt to Income. If Income is zero, a small constant (+1e-5) is added to prevent division by zero.\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights the proportion of income being used to manage debt and is a fundamental metric for assessing financial health. Higher ratios can indicate financial strain, which may affect creditworthiness.\n\ndf['debt_to_income_ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n#### 2. Feature: Credit Score Binning\npython\n# credit_score_bin\n# Feature description: Bins the CreditScore into Low, Medium, and High categories using thresholds: Low (<10), Medium (10–39), and High (>=40).\n# Usefulness: Capturing CreditScore in discrete categories aids interpretability and aligns with common financial risk classifications. It helps the model differentiate groups with diverse credit histories.\n\nbins = [0, 10, 40, float('inf')]\nlabels = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\ndf['credit_score_bin'] = pd.cut(df['CreditScore'], bins=bins, labels=labels)\nend\n\n#### 3. Feature